questions.stem:
Two processes P0 and P1 share `int turn = 0;` and use:

```c
/* Process Pi (i = 0 or 1, j = 1 - i) */
while (true) {
    while (turn != i) ;      /* busy wait */
    /* critical section */
    turn = j;
    /* remainder section */
}
```

P1 wants to enter its critical section repeatedly, but P0, after one pass, stays in its remainder section for a very long time. Which requirement is violated?

Two processes use a shared array `bool flag[2] = {false, false};`:

```c
/* Process Pi, j = 1 - i */
while (true) {
    while (flag[j]) ;      /* wait while the other is interested */
    flag[i] = true;
    /* critical section */
    flag[i] = false;
    /* remainder section */
}
```

Which statement is correct?

Two processes use `bool flag[2] = {false, false};`:

```c
/* Process Pi, j = 1 - i */
while (true) {
    flag[i] = true;
    while (flag[j]) ;      /* wait */
    /* critical section */
    flag[i] = false;
    /* remainder section */
}
```

Which statement is correct?

Two processes use `bool flag[2] = {false, false};`:

```c
/* Process Pi, j = 1 - i */
while (true) {
    flag[i] = true;
    while (flag[j]) {
        flag[i] = false;
        while (flag[j]) ;   /* wait for the other to back off */
        flag[i] = true;
    }
    /* critical section */
    flag[i] = false;
    /* remainder section */
}
```

Take **progress** to mean: whenever no process is in its CS and some want to enter, under **every** possible scheduling one of them eventually enters. Which statement is correct?

Shared integer `x = 0`. Two threads run concurrently:

```c
T1:  for (k = 0; k < 5; k++)  x = x + 1;
T2:  for (k = 0; k < 3; k++)  x = x - 1;
```

Each `x = x ± 1` is a separate load / compute / store, and only these instructions are atomic. How many **distinct** final values of `x` are possible?

Shared integer `x = 1`. Three threads each execute one statement once:

```c
T1:  x = x + 2;
T2:  x = x * 3;
T3:  x = x - 1;
```

Each statement is load / compute / store, and only these instructions are atomic. How many **distinct** final values of `x` are possible?

questions.explanation:
Peterson's algorithm shares:

- `flag[i]` — process i wants to enter;
- `turn` — whose turn it is when both want in.

```c
flag[i] = true; turn = j;
while (flag[j] && turn == j) ;
/* CS */
flag[i] = false;
```

`turn` alone gives strict alternation (fails progress). `flag` alone can deadlock or break ME. Peterson's uses no semaphores; it relies only on atomic loads and stores.

questions.stem:
Peterson's solution:

```c
bool flag[2] = {false, false};
int  turn;

/* Process Pi, j = 1 - i */
while (true) {
    flag[i] = true;
    turn = j;
    while (flag[j] && turn == j) ;   /* busy wait */
    /* critical section */
    flag[i] = false;
    /* remainder section */
}
```

Both processes want to enter at about the same time. The order of shared writes is: P0 sets `flag[0]`, P1 sets `flag[1]`, P0 sets `turn = 1`, P1 sets `turn = 0`. Then both reach their while-tests. What happens?

Peterson's solution:

```c
bool flag[2] = {false, false};
int  turn;

/* Process Pi, j = 1 - i */
while (true) {
    flag[i] = true;
    turn = j;
    while (flag[j] && turn == j) ;   /* busy wait */
    /* critical section */
    flag[i] = false;
    /* remainder section */
}
```

P0 has just executed `turn = 1` and is now in its while loop. What is the **maximum** number of times P1 can **enter** its critical section from this moment until P0 enters?

Peterson's entry section is changed so that `turn` is assigned **before** `flag`:

```c
/* Process Pi, j = 1 - i */
turn = j;
flag[i] = true;
while (flag[j] && turn == j) ;
/* critical section */
flag[i] = false;
```

Initially `flag[0] = flag[1] = false`. Which is correct?

In Peterson's solution the wait condition is changed (assignments unchanged):

```c
/* Process Pi, j = 1 - i */
flag[i] = true;
turn = j;
while (flag[j] && turn == i) ;     /* was: turn == j */
/* critical section */
flag[i] = false;
```

Initially both flags are false and `turn = 0`. Which is correct?

In Peterson's solution `&&` is replaced by `||` in the wait condition:

```c
/* Process Pi, j = 1 - i */
flag[i] = true;
turn = j;
while (flag[j] || turn == j) ;
/* critical section */
flag[i] = false;
```

Initially both flags are false. Which is correct?

Peterson's solution (initially both flags false; `turn` may start with any value):

```c
bool flag[2] = {false, false};
int  turn;

/* Process Pi, j = 1 - i */
while (true) {
    flag[i] = true;
    turn = j;
    while (flag[j] && turn == j) ;   /* busy wait */
    /* critical section */
    flag[i] = false;
    /* remainder section */
}
```

Which statements are correct?

In Peterson's solution the exit section is changed: instead of clearing its flag, a process gives the turn away.

```c
/* Process Pi, j = 1 - i */
while (true) {
    flag[i] = true;
    turn = j;
    while (flag[j] && turn == j) ;
    /* critical section */
    turn = j;                 /* was: flag[i] = false; */
    /* remainder section */
}
```

Initially both flags are false. Which statement is correct?

questions.explanation:
Semantics, executed as **one indivisible** hardware operation:

```c
bool TestAndSet(bool *target) {
    bool rv = *target;
    *target = true;
    return rv;
}
```

A spinlock built from it is `while (TestAndSet(&lock)) ;`. The caller that gets back `false` is the one that found the lock free and now holds it.

questions.stem:
Processes use:

```c
bool lock = false;
/* each process */
while (TestAndSet(&lock)) ;   /* spin */
/* critical section */
lock = false;
```

Which requirements of the critical-section problem does this satisfy (for any number of processes)?

`int CAS(int *p, int expected, int newval)` atomically returns the old value of `*p` and stores `newval` into `*p` **only if** the old value equals `expected`.

```c
int v = 5;
int r1 = CAS(&v, 5, 8);
int r2 = CAS(&v, 5, 9);
int r3 = CAS(&v, 8, 2);
```

What is `r1 + r2 + r3 + v` after these statements?

A programmer writes the spin loop with the test inverted:

```c
bool lock = false;
/* each process */
while (!TestAndSet(&lock)) ;
/* critical section */
lock = false;
```

Which is correct?

Silberschatz's bounded-waiting test-and-set algorithm has this exit section for process Pi among n processes:

```c
j = (i + 1) % n;
while ((j != i) && !waiting[j])
    j = (j + 1) % n;
if (j == i)  lock = false;
else         waiting[j] = false;   /* hand the CS directly to Pj */
```

With n = 6, P2 is leaving its critical section and `waiting[] = {T, F, F, F, T, T}` (indices 0 to 5). Which process enters the critical section next? Give its index.

Consider two locks:

```c
/* Lock X: test-and-test-and-set */
while (true) {
    while (lock) ;                       /* plain read */
    if (!TestAndSet(&lock)) break;
}
/* CS */  lock = false;

/* Lock Y: ticket lock */
int my = FetchAndAdd(&next_ticket, 1);   /* returns old value */
while (now_serving != my) ;
/* CS */  now_serving++;
```

Which statements are correct?

A programmer wants **at most 2** processes in a region at once and uses atomic `FetchAndAdd(int *p, int d)`, which adds `d` to `*p` and returns the **old** value:

```c
int count = 2;

void enter() {
    while (FetchAndAdd(&count, -1) <= 0)   /* no slot: undo and retry */
        FetchAndAdd(&count, +1);
}
void leave() { FetchAndAdd(&count, +1); }
```

Any number of processes call `enter(); /* region */ leave();` repeatedly. Which statements are correct?

A counting semaphore is implemented with a waiting queue:

```c
wait(S):   S.value--;  if (S.value < 0) { add caller to S.list; block(); }
signal(S): S.value++;  if (S.value <= 0) { remove a process P from S.list; wakeup(P); }
```

At some instant `S.value = -3`. What does this tell you?

Statements must run with this precedence: S1 → S2, S1 → S3, S2 → S4, S3 → S4 (S4 only after both S2 and S3). Each Si runs in its own process. All semaphores are counting semaphores initialised to 0.

```c
P1:  S1;  V(a);  V(b);
P2:  P(a);  S2;  V(c);
P3:  P(b);  S3;  ___X___;
P4:  P(c);  ___Y___;  S4;
```

Which choices of (X, Y) enforce the precedence correctly **and** never deadlock?

Two processes use counting semaphores S and Q:

```c
P0:  wait(S);  wait(Q);  /* use */  signal(Q);  signal(S);
P1:  wait(Q);  wait(S);  /* use */  signal(S);  signal(Q);
```

No other process uses S or Q. For which initial values (S, Q) is **deadlock possible**?

A programmer implements `wait` for a binary semaphore **without** making it atomic:

```c
void wait(int *S) {
    while (*S <= 0) ;   /* spin */
    *S = *S - 1;        /* load, subtract, store */
}
void signal(int *S) { *S = *S + 1; }
```

`S = 1` and processes use `wait(&S); CS; signal(&S);`. Which is correct?

Semaphores `a = 0`, `b = 0`. Two processes run concurrently (each `print` is atomic):

```c
P1:  print("A");  signal(a);  print("B");  wait(b);  print("C");
P2:  print("D");  wait(a);    print("E");  signal(b);  print("F");
```

How many **distinct** output strings (of six letters) are possible?

Semaphores `s = 1`, `t = 1`. Two processes run concurrently:

```c
P1:  repeat 3 times { wait(s);  print("A");  signal(t); }
P2:  repeat 3 times { wait(t);  print("B");  signal(s); }
```

How many distinct output strings are possible?

A monitor implements a bounded buffer. The consumer's procedure is:

```c
remove() {
    if (count == 0) notEmpty.wait();
    /* take an item */  count--;
    notFull.signal();
}
```

and `insert()` ends with `count++; notEmpty.signal();`. There may be several consumers. Which statements are correct?

Three processes use semaphores S, T, U, each initialised to 1:

```c
P1:  wait(S); wait(T);  /* work */  signal(T); signal(S);
P2:  wait(T); wait(U);  /* work */  signal(U); signal(T);
P3:  wait(U); wait(S);  /* work */  signal(S); signal(U);
```

Deadlock is possible as written. Which **single** change, on its own, makes deadlock impossible?

Three threads synchronise at a barrier. Shared: `int count = 0; semaphore mutex = 1, barrier = 0;` Each thread executes:

```c
wait(mutex);
count = count + 1;
signal(mutex);
if (count == 3) signal(barrier);   /* count read OUTSIDE the mutex */
wait(barrier);
signal(barrier);
```

All three threads eventually get past the barrier. What is the **maximum** possible value of the semaphore `barrier` after all three have finished?

In the bounded-buffer solution:

```c
semaphore mutex = 1, empty = N, full = 0;

producer:                         consumer:
while (true) {                    while (true) {
    item = produce();                 wait(full);
    wait(empty);                      wait(mutex);
    wait(mutex);                      item = remove();
    insert(item);                     signal(mutex);
    signal(mutex);                    signal(empty);
    signal(full);                     consume(item);
}                                 }
```

Whenever no process is between a `wait` and its matching `signal` on `empty`/`full`, which quantity always equals N?

In the bounded-buffer solution with N = 8:

```c
semaphore mutex = 1, empty = N, full = 0;

producer:                         consumer:
while (true) {                    while (true) {
    item = produce();                 wait(full);
    wait(empty);                      wait(mutex);
    wait(mutex);                      item = remove();
    insert(item);                     signal(mutex);
    signal(mutex);                    signal(empty);
    signal(full);                     consume(item);
}                                 }
```

Starting from the initial state, producers have **completed** 13 insertions and consumers have **completed** 7 removals, and no process is currently inside its loop body. What is the value of `empty`?

In the bounded-buffer solution, the producer's two `wait` operations are swapped:

```c
producer: wait(mutex); wait(empty); insert(item); signal(mutex); signal(full);
consumer: wait(full);  wait(mutex); remove();     signal(mutex); signal(empty);
```

What can go wrong?

Starting from the correct bounded-buffer code:

```c
semaphore mutex = 1, empty = N, full = 0;

producer:                         consumer:
while (true) {                    while (true) {
    item = produce();                 wait(full);
    wait(empty);                      wait(mutex);
    wait(mutex);                      item = remove();
    insert(item);                     signal(mutex);
    signal(mutex);                    signal(empty);
    signal(full);                     consume(item);
}                                 }
```

Which single modification, on its own, can lead to **deadlock**?

Bounded buffer with N = 4 and the standard code:

```c
semaphore mutex = 1, empty = N, full = 0;

producer:                         consumer:
while (true) {                    while (true) {
    item = produce();                 wait(full);
    wait(empty);                      wait(mutex);
    wait(mutex);                      item = remove();
    insert(item);                     signal(mutex);
    signal(mutex);                    signal(empty);
    signal(full);                     consume(item);
}                                 }
```

Semaphores are queue-based (their values may go negative). Starting from the initial state, **only the producer** runs (the consumer has not started). It tries to produce 7 items and runs until it blocks. What is `full + empty` at that point?

A programmer initialises the bounded-buffer semaphores wrongly as `mutex = 1, empty = 0, full = N` (empty and full swapped), with the code otherwise standard:

```c
producer: wait(empty); wait(mutex); insert(); signal(mutex); signal(full);
consumer: wait(full);  wait(mutex); remove(); signal(mutex); signal(empty);
```

The buffer actually starts with no items. What happens?

A circular buffer of N slots is managed without a mutex:

```c
semaphore empty = N, full = 0;   int in = 0, out = 0;

producer: wait(empty); buffer[in] = item; in = (in + 1) % N; signal(full);
consumer: wait(full);  item = buffer[out]; out = (out + 1) % N; signal(empty);
```

(`in = (in + 1) % N` is load / compute / store.) Which statements are correct?

The classic (first) readers–writers solution:

```c
semaphore rw_mutex = 1, mutex = 1;
int read_count = 0;

writer:                          reader:
    wait(rw_mutex);                  wait(mutex);
    /* write */                      read_count++;
    signal(rw_mutex);                if (read_count == 1) wait(rw_mutex);
                                     signal(mutex);
                                     /* read */
                                     wait(mutex);
                                     read_count--;
                                     if (read_count == 0) signal(rw_mutex);
                                     signal(mutex);
```

Which statement is correct?

In the first readers–writers solution:

```c
semaphore rw_mutex = 1, mutex = 1;
int read_count = 0;

writer:                          reader:
    wait(rw_mutex);                  wait(mutex);
    /* write */                      read_count++;
    signal(rw_mutex);                if (read_count == 1) wait(rw_mutex);
                                     signal(mutex);
                                     /* read */
                                     wait(mutex);
                                     read_count--;
                                     if (read_count == 0) signal(rw_mutex);
                                     signal(mutex);
```

What is the purpose of the semaphore `mutex`?

First readers–writers solution:

```c
semaphore rw_mutex = 1, mutex = 1;
int read_count = 0;

writer:                          reader:
    wait(rw_mutex);                  wait(mutex);
    /* write */                      read_count++;
    signal(rw_mutex);                if (read_count == 1) wait(rw_mutex);
                                     signal(mutex);
                                     /* read */
                                     wait(mutex);
                                     read_count--;
                                     if (read_count == 0) signal(rw_mutex);
                                     signal(mutex);
```

A writer is currently writing (`read_count = 0`). Then 5 readers arrive one after another. How many of these readers are blocked on the semaphore `mutex` (as opposed to `rw_mutex`)?

For the first readers–writers solution:

```c
semaphore rw_mutex = 1, mutex = 1;
int read_count = 0;

writer:                          reader:
    wait(rw_mutex);                  wait(mutex);
    /* write */                      read_count++;
    signal(rw_mutex);                if (read_count == 1) wait(rw_mutex);
                                     signal(mutex);
                                     /* read */
                                     wait(mutex);
                                     read_count--;
                                     if (read_count == 0) signal(rw_mutex);
                                     signal(mutex);
```

Which statements are correct?

A programmer moves `signal(mutex)` in the reader's entry section to **before** the test:

```c
reader:
    wait(mutex);
    read_count++;
    signal(mutex);
    if (read_count == 1) wait(rw_mutex);    /* tested outside mutex */
    /* read */
    wait(mutex);
    read_count--;
    if (read_count == 0) signal(rw_mutex);
    signal(mutex);

writer:  wait(rw_mutex); /* write */ signal(rw_mutex);
```

Which is a possible consequence?

In this readers–writers code the reader's **exit** test is done outside `mutex`. All semaphores are **counting** semaphores (a signal always increments), with `mutex = 1`, `wrt = 1`, `rc = 0`.

```c
reader:                                  writer:
    wait(mutex);                             wait(wrt);
    rc = rc + 1;                             /* write */
    if (rc == 1) wait(wrt);                  signal(wrt);
    signal(mutex);
    /* read */
    wait(mutex);
    rc = rc - 1;
    signal(mutex);
    if (rc == 0) signal(wrt);   /* tested outside mutex */
```

Any number of readers and writers may arrive. Which statements are correct?

Five philosophers use this solution:

```c
semaphore chopstick[5] = {1, 1, 1, 1, 1};

/* philosopher i */
while (true) {
    wait(chopstick[i]);               /* left  */
    wait(chopstick[(i + 1) % 5]);     /* right */
    eat();
    signal(chopstick[(i + 1) % 5]);
    signal(chopstick[i]);
    think();
}
```

What is its main problem?

Five philosophers run the naive solution, with queue-based semaphores (values may go negative):

```c
semaphore chopstick[5] = {1, 1, 1, 1, 1};

/* philosopher i */
while (true) {
    wait(chopstick[i]);               /* left  */
    wait(chopstick[(i + 1) % 5]);     /* right */
    eat();
    signal(chopstick[(i + 1) % 5]);
    signal(chopstick[i]);
    think();
}
```

The system reaches the deadlock in which every philosopher holds its left chopstick and is blocked on its right one. What is the **sum** of the values of the five `chopstick` semaphores?

Peterson's solution for processes P0 and P1 (j = 1 − i) is:

```c
bool flag[2] = {false, false};  int turn;      /* turn may start as 0 or 1 */
/* entry */  flag[i] = true;  turn = j;  while (flag[j] && turn == j) ;
/* critical section */
/* exit  */  flag[i] = false;
```

Assume loads and stores are atomic and execute in program order. **Progress** here means: if no process is in its CS and some want to enter, one of them will be able to enter, and a process in its remainder section can never stop the other from entering.

Each variant changes only what is shown (everything else as above). Which variants guarantee **both** mutual exclusion and progress?

A programmer "generalises" Peterson's solution to **four** processes P0 to P3:

```c
bool flag[4] = {false, false, false, false};
int  turn = 0;

/* process Pi */
while (true) {
    flag[i] = true;
    turn = i;
    while ((flag[a] || flag[b] || flag[c]) && turn == i) ;   /* a, b, c = the other three */
    /* critical section */
    flag[i] = false;
    /* remainder section */
}
```

Loads and stores are atomic and execute in program order. What is the **maximum** number of processes that can be in the critical section at the same time?

`CAS(&x, e, n)` atomically compares `x` with `e`; if equal it stores `n`. It always returns the old value of `x`. A lock-free increment is:

```c
void increment() {
    int old;
    do {
        old = x;                                  /* atomic load */
    } while (CAS(&x, old, old + 1) != old);       /* retry if x changed */
}
```

Shared `x = 0`. Threads T1 and T2 each call `increment()` **3 times**. Over all interleavings, what is the **maximum** total number of CAS calls that **fail**?

A counting semaphore with initial value K is implemented from two **binary** semaphores (a `signal` on a binary semaphore whose value is already 1 leaves it at 1):

```c
int c = K;
binary_semaphore mutex = 1, delay = 0;

void P() {
    wait(mutex);
    c = c - 1;
    if (c < 0) { signal(mutex); wait(delay); }
    else       signal(mutex);
}

void V() {
    wait(mutex);
    c = c + 1;
    if (c <= 0) signal(delay);
    signal(mutex);
}
```

Which statements are correct?

First readers–writers solution with **queue-based** semaphores (a `wait` that finds the value ≤ 0 still decrements it and blocks; queues are FIFO):

```c
semaphore rw_mutex = 1, mutex = 1;   int read_count = 0;

writer:  wait(rw_mutex);  /* write */  signal(rw_mutex);

reader:  wait(mutex);  read_count++;
         if (read_count == 1) wait(rw_mutex);
         signal(mutex);
         /* read */
         wait(mutex);  read_count--;
         if (read_count == 0) signal(rw_mutex);
         signal(mutex);
```

Events in order (each process runs until it starts reading/writing, blocks, or finishes; a woken process continues the same way):

1. R1 arrives  2. W1 arrives  3. R2 arrives  4. R1 finishes reading  5. W2 arrives  6. R2 finishes reading  7. R3 arrives  8. R4 arrives

What is `rw_mutex + mutex + read_count` after event 8?

The first readers–writers solution is:

```c
semaphore rw_mutex = 1, mutex = 1;   int read_count = 0;

writer:  wait(rw_mutex);  /* write */  signal(rw_mutex);

reader:  wait(mutex);  read_count++;
         if (read_count == 1) wait(rw_mutex);
         signal(mutex);
         /* read */
         wait(mutex);  read_count--;
         if (read_count == 0) signal(rw_mutex);
         signal(mutex);
```

Each variant changes **only the reader** as shown (writers unchanged). Any number of readers and writers may run repeatedly. Which variants are still correct, i.e. a writer never overlaps any other reader or writer, readers may read together, and no deadlock is possible?

A producer and a consumer share a buffer of N slots (N > 2) using `sleep()` and `wakeup()` instead of semaphores. `sleep()` blocks the caller. `wakeup(p)` unblocks p if p is asleep and **does nothing** otherwise.

```c
int count = 0;                        /* items in buffer */

producer:                             consumer:
while (true) {                        while (true) {
    item = produce();                     if (count == 0) sleep();
    if (count == N) sleep();              item = remove();
    insert(item);                         count = count - 1;
    count = count + 1;                    if (count == N - 1) wakeup(producer);
    if (count == 1) wakeup(consumer);     consume(item);
}                                     }
```

The buffer starts empty. Which execution order leaves **both** processes asleep forever?
